@page "/HowItWorks/short-sales"
@using InvestmentTaxCalculator.Components

<HowItWorksGuideLayout PageAddress="HowItWorks/short-sales">
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="section">
            <p>
                Sometimes a sale is still not fully matched after the <a href="HowItWorks/same-day-rule" class="link-styled">same-day rule</a>,
                the <a href="HowItWorks/30-day-rule" class="link-styled">30-day rule</a> and the
                <a href="HowItWorks/section-104-pool" class="link-styled">Section 104 pool</a>, because you sold more than you
                held. The rest of the sale waits for later purchases:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    It is matched with later acquisitions, earliest first (TCGA 1992 s105(2)). A purchase within 30 days is
                    matched by the 30-day rule first, so this only applies to purchases after that window.
                </li>
                <li>These matches are labelled "Cover short selling" on the <a href="TradeMatchPage" class="link-styled">Trade Matches</a> page.</li>
                <li>
                    Until a later purchase is imported, the unmatched shares are left out of the gain, and the
                    <a href="CalculationViewPage" class="link-styled">Disposal Summary</a> shows them as unmatched shares.
                    The gain counts in the tax year of the sale.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Options and futures follow the same matching steps, with some extra rules described in the
                <a href="https://github.com/alexpung/UK-Investment-tax-calculator/wiki/Traded-Financial-Options" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">options</a> and
                <a href="https://github.com/alexpung/UK-Investment-tax-calculator/wiki/Future-Contracts" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">futures</a> wiki pages.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card mb-4">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h5 card-title">Worked example</h2>
                <p>
                    With no shares held, you sell 100 shares on 5 May 2024 for £1,000 less a £5 fee (net £995). On
                    6 December 2024 you buy 100 shares for £500 plus a £5 fee (£505).
                </p>
                <p class="mb-0">
                    The December purchase is more than 30 days after the sale, so it is matched as cover for the short sale:
                    £995 − £505 = a <strong>gain of £490</strong>, in the 2024/25 tax year. Nothing is left in the pool.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
    <FurtherReading>
        <li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/capital-gains-manual/cg51555" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HMRC CG51555</a>: the share identification order</li>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/alexpung/UK-Investment-tax-calculator/wiki/Share-matching" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Project wiki: share matching</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1992/12/section/105" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TCGA 1992 s105</a></li>
    </FurtherReading>
</HowItWorksGuideLayout>
